; @AGILELENS 2026-08-30: EXPERIMENTAL, testing a hypothesis from an offline .gputrace analysis
; (scripts/gputrace/gputrace.py) of this exact build (com.acp.MyProject on Alex's M5). Reported
; symptom: "wonky and zoomed" stereo -- one eye reads at a dramatically different scale/framing
; than the other. Dumped SceneColor (the app's own render target, pre-copy) and it is CORRECTLY
; matched between the two eye slices -- normal small stereo parallax only. Dumped Color_SwapChain0/1
; (the actual OpenXR-submitted output) and the same two eyes show the scale/zoom mismatch. So the
; corruption is introduced AFTER the app's own rendering, in the encoder trace's
; warpPreProcess/VFWMeshGeneration/StreamReduceDepthBin-L/R chain -- Apple's own visionOS system
; compositor (wakeboardd) foveated-warp/reprojection pass, which builds its per-eye warp mesh from
; the depth the app submits via the OpenXR depth extension. This project never had
; xr.OpenXRAllowDepthLayer set at all (unlike the sibling pinchwork-avp/PinchworkTemplate project,
; which already disables it for an unrelated crash -- see that project's DefaultEngine.ini). Testing
; whether disabling depth submission here also eliminates the per-eye warp corruption, on the theory
; that malformed/inconsistent per-eye depth is confusing the system compositor's reprojection, not
; UE's own renderer. UNVERIFIED until re-traced on device -- if the zoom mismatch persists with this
; set to False, the depth-submission theory is wrong and this should be reverted.
; VERDICT 2026-08-31: I reverted this experiment, per its own instruction above. My call, on evidence.
; It was never evaluated for the zoom/warp theory because it caused a HARD CRASH first.
; Symbolicated terminal stack from the on-device crash report (Pinchwork Fork 0.0.4):
;   FDebug::CheckVerifyFailedImpl2 -> FOXRVisionOSSession::XrEndFrame -> FOpenXRHMD::OnFinishRendering
;   -> FOpenXRRenderBridge::Present -> FMetalRHICommandContext::EndDrawingViewport
; XrEndFrame (OXRVisionOSSession.cpp:1231) has an unconditional `check(Depth0 && Depth1)` that does
; NOT consult this cvar. Disabling depth leaves the swapchain unbound -- the app log shows
; "[MSAAHUNT] Depth.Resolve = TRANSIENT texture -- XR depth swapchain NOT bound" twice immediately
; before the critical error -- and then that check asserts it IS bound. Disabling depth here does not
; avoid a crash, it causes one.
; Corroborating device observation: pinchwork-vanilla carried the identical line; another session
; removed it, and that build now launches and behaves correctly in the headset. This restores the
; same known-good state on the fork side.
; The zoom/warp depth-submission theory is therefore still UNTESTED, not disproven -- if it is
; retried, it needs a path that does not trip XrEndFrame's assert.
;xr.OpenXRAllowDepthLayer=False
